  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1047 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAs of 8:30 PM this evening, Isaias made landfall along the Gulf\nCoast as a Category 2 hurricane. We have already seen cloud\ncoverage increase across portions of the Mid-South, with coverage\nbecoming more widespread through early Saturday morning. Looking\nat observations to our southeast, some heavy rain bands are\nalready moving through Central Alabama with wind speeds already\nincreasing. As things stand currently, gusty winds will begin to\nmove into the Mid-South from the southeast around 4 AM, spreading\nacross the area through Saturday morning. A Tropical Storm\nWarning remains in effect for a small portion of northeast\nMississippi, with all other locations across the Mid-South having\na Wind Advisory from 4 AM until 8 PM on Saturday.  Latest HREF\nguidance has a very high probability (>90%) for most locations\neast of the Mississippi River experiencing wind gusts over 40 mph\nwith a medium chance (40 to 60%) of these gusts approaching\n50 mph especially over northeast Mississippi. While the main\ncirculation is expected to remain just to our east, gusty winds\nappear likely for the area. Given the likelihood of sustained\nwinds between 20 to 25 mph and wind gusts up to 50 mph, and the\nprolonged nature, there will likely be at least some minor wind\nimpacts throughout the day across most of the area.\n\nIn addition to the strong wind threat, rain bands will move\nthrough throughout the day on Saturday. With the rain threat,\nthere is still significant uncertainty as to if dry air will be\nable to infiltrate the system, which could lower our total\nrainfall amounts. There will likely be a sharp cutoff in total\nrainfall amounts on the western edge of the system, mainly along\nand west of the Mississippi River where totals will likely fall\nbelow an inch. For locations along the MS/AL line and along the\nTN River, 1 to 3 inches remains likely. With the latest guidance\ncontinuing to show a downward trend in precipitation along the\nMississippi River, opted to remove some counties from the current\nwatch where precipitation totals will likely fall below an inch.\nWe may be able to destabilize a bit through the morning with a\nfew embedded thunderstorms that could boost rainfall totals,\notherwise a much needed rainfall will be what most of the area\nsees. The bulk of any strong winds and precipitation will begin\nto move out by Saturday evening, with much of it moving out of\nthe area by midnight.\n\nBy Sunday, the parent low of Isaias will be fully enveloped with\nthe upper-level flow regime, making a quick exit to the northeast\naway from the region. As such, expect for dry conditions by the\nmorning hours, with some warm air beginning to filter in behind\nthe system. With lingering cloudiness across the east, expect for\na sharp temperature gradient across the Mid-South with locations\nalong the TN River likely remaining in the mid 70s while\nlocations west of the MS River will likely climb to the upper 80s\nto low 90s. High pressure will slowly build across the region\ninto the middle of next week, leading to a continued dry weather\npattern and temperatures returning to unseasonably warm. High\ntemperatures will span the upper 80s to lower 90s across most of\nthe area through at least the middle of next week. By the middle\nto end of next week, high pressure will begin to push eastward\nwith a low pressure system moving off the Rockies. There are\nstill significant differences between ensemble guidance heading\ninto the next week, leading to little confidence in the overall\nlong term forecast.",
